---
// Bead history and durability — the payoff under rung two of the ladder, not a
// new rung. Backfill (the safety envelope), the BeadLog archive, the opt-in
// XTDB store, and native backups proven by restoring them.
//
// Register (data.astro): ICP-2 facing, "nothing is abandoned first". Every
// claim below is sourced from the beadhive docs listed in `sources`; default-
// off capabilities are named as opt-in, and no version numbers appear.
const sources = [
  { label: 'Backfill: passes, plans, the safety envelope', href: 'https://github.com/beadhive/beadhive/blob/main/docs/BACKFILL.md' },
  { label: 'The XTDB store plugin', href: 'https://github.com/beadhive/beadhive/blob/main/docs/XTDB-STORE.md' },
  { label: 'Backfill platform ADR', href: 'https://github.com/beadhive/beadhive/blob/main/docs/design/backfill-platform-adr.md' },
  { label: 'BeadLog format ADR', href: 'https://github.com/beadhive/beadhive/blob/main/docs/design/beadlog-format-adr.md' },
  { label: 'Backup retention boundary ADR', href: 'https://github.com/beadhive/beadhive/blob/main/docs/design/backup-retention-boundary-adr.md' },
  { label: 'Upgrading: install extras', href: 'https://github.com/beadhive/beadhive/blob/main/docs/UPGRADING.md' },
];
---
<section id="history">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">Under rung two</div>
    <h2 class="display">What the graph remembers — and what keeps it safe</h2>
    <p>A bead store holds the <em>current</em> state of each bead. History is what erodes: the
    tracker's own event log starts well after the first beads existed, and a merge that landed
    months ago is a fact only git still knows. A graph that can't say <em>when</em> a decision
    was made, or which commits delivered it, is one the planner can't fully trust. Three things
    close that gap, and a fourth keeps the whole store recoverable.</p>

    <div class="cardgrid hist-grid">
      <div class="card"><h4>Backfill <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></h4>
        <p>Brings a hive's beads in line with its history: links commits to the beads they
        delivered, links decision docs to the beads they belong to, derives when each lifecycle
        stage happened, and can propose beads from a planning tree. It keeps <em>when it
        happened</em> apart from <em>when it was recorded</em>, and marks how sure it is.
        Run it twice and the second run proposes nothing.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>BeadLog <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></h4>
        <p>A portable archive of bead history events — a <code>.bdlg</code> file that carries its
        own schemas and rebuilds byte-for-byte. The same file is backfill's plan: a pass writes a
        proposal, reconcile sorts it against what's already there, apply loads it. An early,
        versioned format, not a standard.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>XTDB store <span class="status shipped">shipped</span> <b class="optin">opt-in</b></h4>
        <p>An optional plugin that loads a BeadLog into a database keeping both of those times
        queryable. You install the extra, enable the plugin, and run the database yourself —
        <code>bh</code> never starts or stops it. The BeadLog stays the source of truth; losing
        the XTDB copy costs a reload, never data.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Backups <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></h4>
        <p>A snapshot of the hive's store that is not trusted until it has been restored into a
        scratch database and its fingerprint matched the live one. You run it; nothing takes
        snapshots for you on a schedule.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="display">The safety envelope, in plain terms</h3>
    <p>Every backfill pass previews by default and writes nothing until you re-run it with
    <code>--apply</code> against a plan you have saved and looked at. The apply itself is
    wrapped, in this order:</p>
    <ol class="envelope">
      <li><b>Sealed plan.</b> The plan is hashed when it's written; an edited plan is refused.</li>
      <li><b>Drift gate.</b> If the hive changed since the plan was made, the apply is refused.</li>
      <li><b>Loss report.</b> The store says up front what it cannot keep.</li>
      <li><b>Recovery first.</b> A recovery export of the touched beads is written before the
      first write, and an audit records every step.</li>
      <li><b>Proof after.</b> The result must equal the expected post-state, and a second dry run
      must propose nothing, before the apply is reported done.</li>
      <li><b>Rollback.</b> On any failure the writes are undone in reverse. Backfill never
      deletes a bead — undoing a create retires it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Docs it can't match by rule are left for you to confirm; nothing is created for them.
    A <b>judge</b> can suggest matches for those leftovers, but it is off by default, you supply
    the model and the key, and every verdict is recorded and marked for review. That judge is
    the one place in this area where "<code>bh</code> makes no model call" needs the qualifier:
    with it off, nothing is called. Keeping linkage current after each merge is likewise
    available and off by default — bounded, best effort, and unable to fail a merge. A
    <b>coverage</b> report says how well work is linked, and marks stale figures stale rather
    than presenting them as current.</p>

    <h3 class="display">The restore-proof</h3>
    <div class="hist-flow">
      <div class="term">
        <div class="term-bar"><span class="term-dot"></span>bh — zsh</div>
<pre><code><span class="p">$</span> bh backup snapshot                <span class="c"># take one dated set; restore it into scratch; compare</span>
<span class="p">$</span> bh backup list                    <span class="c"># every set, and whether it was proven</span>
<span class="p">$</span> bh backup verify --restore-test   <span class="c"># prove a set again, later</span>
<span class="p">$</span> bh backup restore --dry-run       <span class="c"># preview; a real run needs --confirm &lt;hive&gt;</span></code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="hist-flow-text">
        <p>A snapshot is one dated set: the issues as JSONL, a native database backup, the
        registry of remotes, and a manifest. Before success is reported, the native backup is
        restored into a uniquely named scratch database and its full fingerprint must equal the
        live store's; a set that was not restored is listed as unverified. The scratch database
        is dropped afterwards (the database engine keeps a copy it names in the report; nothing
        is purged behind your back).</p>
        <p>Restoring over a live hive is never implicit: a preview first, the hive's name typed
        to confirm, and a safety snapshot of what is about to be replaced — if that snapshot
        fails, the restore refuses with zero change.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note"><b>What stays yours.</b> BeadLog archives hold private bead content and
    live in a private host directory — never committed, never pushed. The XTDB node listens on
    loopback only, with a password that lives in your secret broker, never in config. Backfill's
    switches are host-owned: a fleet push cannot turn the judge, the gap fill, or continuous
    backfill on.</div>

    <p class="srcs-title">Sources</p>
    <ul class="srcs">{sources.map((s) => <li><a href={s.href}>{s.label} ↗</a></li>)}</ul>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hist-grid { margin: 22px 0 30px; }
  .card h4 .optin {
    font-family: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 10px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  h3.display { margin: 28px 0 10px; font-size: 20px; }
  .envelope { margin: 10px 0 16px; padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .envelope li { margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; }
  .envelope b { color: var(--honey-strong); font-weight: 650; }
  .hist-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 18px; margin: 10px 0 24px; }
  .hist-flow .term { max-width: none; }
  .hist-flow-text p + p { margin-top: 10px; }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .hist-flow { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.15fr) minmax(0, 1fr); align-items: start; }
  }
  .srcs-title {
    margin: 26px 0 6px;
    font-family: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .srcs { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: 14px; }
  .srcs li { margin: 3px 0; }
</style>
